  - States reproduced from the web source: Ready (RGB/HSL/HEX result grid, the web {parsed && ...}) + Empty
    (incomplete/invalid hex). The web source has NO loading/error/stale/offline branch (it derives the result
    synchronously from local hex text via useMemo, with no network read), so none were fabricated -- parity,
    not invention (same precedent as the sibling ClientUtilitiesSection). The web renders nothing when the hex
    is invalid; the native surface renders a friendly TsEmptyState instead (this prompt's required empty state
    + engineering guideline #6 "never hide a section behind null"). Additive, documented -- not silent drift.
  - rgbToHsl ported exactly from helpers.ts; JavaScript Math.round reproduced as floor(x+0.5); parseInt(_,16)
    reproduced faithfully (leading-whitespace trim, optional sign, optional 0x prefix, greedy hex run, NaN only
    when no digit consumed -- so '3g82f6' parses to rgb(3,130,246) exactly as the web does); replace('#','')
    removes only the FIRST '#'. The result strings match the web template strings: rgb(r, g, b) / hsl(h, s%, l%)
    / the echoed hex. Asserted against white/black/red/green/blue/grey + the #3b82f6 web sample.
  - i18n: every label flows through ILocalizer with the exact web keys -- t('Color Converter'),
    t('Color Converter Desc'), t('Hex Color') (key == English fallback, i18next default-value style) + the
    shared CopyButton keys common.copyButton.copy / common.copyButton.copied; the native-only empty-state and
    Narrator-name keys carry English fallbacks. No hardcoded UI copy (RGB/HSL/HEX are the web's own untranslated
    format acronyms, kept as constants -- localizing them would be drift FROM the source).
  - a11y: AutomationProperties.Name on the hex field (its label), the colour swatch (named for the current hex)
    and every copy button (scoped to its format); the conversion result is announced through a polite live
    region; the empty surface (TsEmptyState) is its own live region; no custom motion is added, so reduced-motion
    is honoured by construction. Surface identity (Palette glyph \uE790, neon-purple accent TsColorAccentBrush)
    matches the catalog entry the sibling ClientUtilitiesSection already registers for the 'color' tool.
  - telemetry: ColorConverterDiagnostics.RecordViewOpened() emits 'view.opened slug=ColorConverter' (P1/S11),
    PII-safe (never the hex or the derived colour).
  - No direct HTTP in the view; the only web data source is useTranslation (ILocalizer). The colour computation
    is local + pure (ColorMath / ColorConverterProjection), bound through the WinUI-free state holder (P1/S8).
    The swatch is tinted from the parsed ARGB channels (a WinUI brush needs a structured colour; the web's
    arbitrary CSS-string background cannot map 1:1), and is neutral when the hex is invalid.

